==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[sex#Old English|sex]]
* [[syx#Old English|syx]]

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|ang}} {{recons|sehs|lang=gem-pro}}, whence also Old Frisian {{term|sex|lang=ofs}}, Old Saxon {{term|sehs|lang=osx}}, Middle Dutch {{term|sesse}} (Dutch {{term|zes|lang=nl}}), Old High German {{term|sehs|lang=goh}} (German {{term|sechs}}), Old Norse [[sex]] (Swedish [[sex]]), Gothic {{term|𐍃𐌰𐌹𐌷𐍃|tr=saihs|lang=got}}. Ultimately from {{etyl|ine-pro|ang}} {{recons|swéḱs|lang=ine-pro}}, cognate with Greek {{term|ἕξ|tr=héx|lang=grc}}, Latin {{term|sex|lang=la}}, Old Irish {{term|se|sē|lang=sga}} (Irish {{term|sé|lang=ga}}), Old Slavic ''šestʼ'' (Russian {{term|шесть|tr=šestʼ|lang=ru}}).

===Pronunciation===
